course: dictionary
lesson: 11631
title: Old words and old things
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Archaic describes something very old, from the distant past. It can also describe something old-fashioned that is no longer used, or is too old to be useful. Look for clues in the words around it, like «hundreds of years ago» or «out of date».
explain: «The museum had an archaic map of the town, drawn hundreds of years ago.» The clue is hundreds of years ago, so archaic must mean very old. Try your meaning in the sentence: very old map, and it still makes sense.
explain: Old texts often contain archaic words that people no longer use in everyday talk. In «The knight said, «Thou shalt not pass»», thou is an old word for you. Words like thou, thee and hath are archaic, so you would meet them in old stories, not in a chat with a friend today.
explain: Archaic is an adjective, so it describes a noun, like archaic language, archaic rules or an archaic computer. You can compare it too: archaic, more archaic, most archaic.
fact: Archaic means very old, from the distant past.
fact: Archaic also means old-fashioned and no longer used, or too old to be useful.
fact: In English lessons, archaic words include thou, thee and hath, which you find in old texts.
fact: «An archaic computer» means a computer that is too old to be useful today.
fact: Archaic is an adjective, so you can say archaic language, archaic word or archaic system.
exercise: Mia finds a map drawn five hundred years ago. What does archaic mean here? | Written in a foreign language | *Very old, from the distant past | Brand new and shiny | Very small
exercise: Which word is an archaic word from old English texts? | television | *hath | pizza | bicycle
exercise: A teacher says, «Your phone is archaic, it is ten years out of date.» What does archaic suggest? | *Old-fashioned and no longer useful | Very fast and modern | Brand new | Made of gold
test: «The old castle had archaic rules that nobody followed any more.» What does archaic show? | *Old-fashioned and no longer used | Kind and friendly | Very loud | Clever and modern
test: Which best describes archaic language? | *Words like thou and hath used in old texts | Words from today's news | Text messages and emojis | Slang you hear at the football match
exam: Which sentence uses archaic language correctly? | The shop sells fresh bread every morning. | I will text you later about the match. | We played football at break time. | *Thou art my dearest friend, said the old king.
